var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"scripting/#Working-from-Julia-code","page":"Working from Julia code","text":"Everything the window does is also available as functions. Structures are immutable Crystal values, and editing functions return new ones. The examples on this page run when the documentation is built, so their output is real. They use the example files that come with the package:\n\nusing AtomBond\nexample(name) = joinpath(pkgdir(AtomBond), \"examples\", \"structures\", name)\nsi = load_structure(example(\"Si_conv.vasp\"))\nCrystal Si8  \"Si diamond, conventional cell (a = 5.431 A)\"\n  Lattice(a=5.4310, b=5.4310, c=5.4310 Å, α=90.00, β=90.00, γ=90.00°)\n  volume = 160.1915 Å³, pbc = (true, true, true)\n  Si    0.000000   0.000000   0.000000\n  Si    0.000000   0.500000   0.500000\n  Si    0.500000   0.000000   0.500000\n  Si    0.500000   0.500000   0.000000\n  Si    0.250000   0.250000   0.250000\n  Si    0.250000   0.750000   0.750000\n  Si    0.750000   0.250000   0.750000\n  Si    0.750000   0.750000   0.250000\n","title":"Working from Julia code"},{"category":"section","location":"scripting/#Structure-and-symmetry","page":"Working from Julia code","text":"natoms(si), formula(si), lattice_parameters(si.lattice)\n(8, \"Si8\", (5.431, 5.431, 5.431, 90.0, 90.0, 90.0))\n\nrutile = load_structure(example(\"TiO2_rutile.cif\"))     # symmetry-expanded CIF\nspacegroup(rutile)\n(136, \"P4_2/mnm\")\n\nnatoms(primitive_cell(si)), natoms(conventional_cell(si))\n(2, 8)\n\nnatoms(supercell(si, 2, 2, 1)), natoms(supercell(si, [1 -1 0; 1 1 0; 0 0 1]))\n(32, 16)","title":"Structure and symmetry"},{"category":"section","location":"scripting/#Bonds-and-measurements","page":"Working from Julia code","text":"bonds = find_bonds(si)\nlength(bonds), round(bonds[1].length; digits=4)\n(16, 2.3517)\n\nr = cartesian_positions(si)\nb = first(filter(b -> b.i == 1, bonds))             # a bond of atom 1\nround(distance(r[1], r[b.j] + si.lattice.matrix * b.shift); digits=4)\n2.3517\n\nwater = load_library(\"Water\")\np = cartesian_positions(water)                        # O, H, H\nround(bond_angle(p[2], p[1], p[3]); digits=2)         # H–O–H angle\n104.48\n\nround(min_image_distance(si, 1, 8); digits=4)         # shortest periodic distance\n2.3517","title":"Bonds and measurements"},{"category":"section","location":"scripting/#Editing","page":"Working from Julia code","text":"sige = substitute(si, [5], :Ge)\nformula(sige)\n\"Si7Ge1\"\n\nc = delete_atoms(si, [1])\nc = move_atoms(c, [4], [0.1, 0, 0])                   # Cartesian Å\nc = add_atom(c, :O, [0.5, 0.5, 0.5])                  # fractional\nformula(c)\n\"Si7O1\"\n\ngaas = load_library(\"GaAs\")\nformula(replace_species(gaas, :As, :P))\n\"Ga4P4\"\n\ngaas2 = set_position(gaas, 5, (0.3, 0.25, 0.25))\ngaas2.frac[5]\n3-element StaticArraysCore.SVector{3, Float64} with indices SOneTo(3):\n 0.3\n 0.25\n 0.25\n\nshifted = translate_atoms(si, [0.5, 0, 0]; fractional=true)\nlength(atoms_outside_cell(shifted)), length(atoms_outside_cell(wrap(shifted)))\n(2, 0)","title":"Editing"},{"category":"section","location":"scripting/#Surfaces-and-superlattices","page":"Working from Julia code","text":"s = slab(si, (1, 1, 1); layers=4, vacuum=15)          # termination chosen automatically\nnatoms(s), s.pbc\n(32, (true, true, true))\n\nge = Crystal(cubic(5.658), fill(:Ge, 8), si.frac)\nsl = superlattice(si, ge, (0, 0, 1); layers=(4, 4), strain=true)\nformula(sl)\n\"Si32Ge32\"","title":"Surfaces and superlattices"},{"category":"section","location":"scripting/#Constraints-and-DFT-inputs","page":"Working from Julia code","text":"relax = with_constraints(fix_atoms(s, 1:4); cell=(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0))\ncount(f -> all(f), relax.fixed), describe_cell_constraints(relax.cell_constraints)\n(4, \"cell fixed\")\n\nkpoint_grid(si; kspacing=0.25)\n(5, 5, 5)\n\ndir = mktempdir()\nwrite_qe(joinpath(dir, \"si.in\"), si; ecutwfc=40, pseudo=\"{El}.UPF\")\nwrite_abinit(joinpath(dir, \"si.abi\"), si; ecutwfc=40, pseudo=\"{El}.psp8\")\nwrite_siesta(joinpath(dir, \"si.fdf\"), si)\nreaddir(dir)\n3-element Vector{String}:\n \"si.abi\"\n \"si.fdf\"\n \"si.in\"\n\nsave_structure(joinpath(dir, \"SiGe.cif\"), sige)\nformula(load_structure(joinpath(dir, \"SiGe.cif\")))\n\"Si7Ge1\"\n\nframes = read_castep_geom_frames(example(\"Si_relax.geom\"))   # every step of a relaxation\nlength(frames), first(frames).title\n(2, \"Si_relax.geom, step 0, E = -391.327295 eV\")","title":"Constraints and DFT inputs"},{"category":"section","location":"scripting/#The-window-from-code","page":"Working from Julia code","text":"These open a window, so they are not run when the documentation is built:\n\nv = visualize(si)                               # window with the control panel\nview_along!(v, :c); view_along!(v, [1, 1, 0])   # a, b, c, a*, b*, c* or [uvw]\nv.settings.bond_stubs = false; redraw!(v)       # any setting, then redraw\nsave_view(\"si_view.png\", v)                     # just the 3D view\nsnapshot(\"si.png\", si; settings=ViewSettings(style=:cpk))   # PNG, no window\n\nselect!(v, 5); select!(v, 1; add=true)          # same as click / Shift+click\nv.info[]                                        # \"Si5 – Si1   distance 2.3517 Å\"\nedit!(c -> substitute(c, [5], :Ge), v, \"make SiGe\")   # undoable in the window\nundo!(v); redo!(v)\nset_crystal!(v, supercell(si, 2, 2, 2))         # show another structure, not undoable\nnew_tab!(v, load_library(\"GaN\"))                # a second structure in a new tab","title":"The window from code"},{"category":"section","location":"building/#Building-and-editing","page":"Building and editing","text":"All of these are undoable steps in the window, and functions that return a new Crystal in code (see Working from Julia code).","title":"Building and editing"},{"category":"section","location":"building/#New-structure","page":"Building and editing","text":"File → New structure… starts from an empty cell (a b c, α β γ; then add atoms with Add) or a prototype filled with your elements and lattice constants: simple, body-centred and face-centred cubic, diamond, zincblende, rocksalt, CsCl, fluorite, perovskite ABX₃, hcp, wurtzite. Cubic prototypes take a, hexagonal ones a and c. The new structure opens in a new tab and is \"Untitled\" until saved. In code: prototype_crystal.","title":"New structure"},{"category":"section","location":"building/#Editing-atoms","page":"Building and editing","text":"The panel's Edit selected atoms row replaces the selected atoms by another element, deletes them (also the Delete key), moves them by a Cartesian vector in Å, or adds an atom at a fractional position. Atoms… opens the Edit atoms tool: for one selected atom, change its element and set its exact position (fractional or Cartesian); for several, change all their elements at once. Its Replace a species everywhere section turns every atom of one element into another (e.g. all As → P).\n\nChanging elements keeps the bonds. After As → C in GaAs the Ga–C distance is still 2.45 Å, longer than the usual Ga–C limit, so the new pair inherits the old pair's limit and the status line says so (ViewSettings.bond_cutoffs).\n\nIn code: substitute, delete_atoms, move_atoms, add_atom, set_position, replace_species.","title":"Editing atoms"},{"category":"section","location":"building/#Supercells","page":"Building and editing","text":"Build → Supercell… makes repeats n1 n2 n3, or any cell whose vectors a′ b′ c′ are integer combinations of a, b, c (the columns of the supercell matrix). The Type menu has presets: √2×√2 R45° (square ab), √3×√3 R30° and rectangular 1×√3 (hexagonal ab). A preview shows the multiplicity, atom count and new cell. Primitive and Conventional (panel or Build menu) use spglib; Original goes back to the structure as opened. In code: supercell, primitive_cell, conventional_cell.","title":"Supercells"},{"category":"section","location":"building/#Shift,-rotate-and-wrap","page":"Building and editing","text":"Shift atoms moves all atoms, or the selected ones, by a vector in fractional or Cartesian coordinates. Selected atom → origin fills in the shift that puts the selected atom at (0, 0, 0). Atoms that end up outside the cell are drawn where they are, not folded back in.\n\nRotate atoms turns all atoms, or the selected ones, by an angle about an axis through a centre; the cell does not turn.\n\nAxis: a Cartesian direction, a lattice direction [u v w], the line from the first to the second selected atom (this also fixes the centre), or the viewing direction.\nCentre: the centroid of the rotated atoms, the first selected atom, or a point.\nPositive angles turn counterclockwise when the axis points toward you. Selected atoms turn as the copies you clicked, so a group that crosses a cell face turns as one piece.\nThe preview names the closest contact afterwards and flags it below 0.6 × the sum of covalent radii.\n\nWrap atoms into cell moves atoms outside the cell back in by whole lattice vectors, so fractional coordinates end up in [0, 1); the periodic structure does not change. Shift and Rotate have a switch to wrap afterwards. ViewSettings(wrap_atoms=true) draws every atom inside the cell regardless.\n\nIn code: translate_atoms, rotate_atoms, rotation_matrix, closest_contact, wrap, atoms_outside_cell.","title":"Shift, rotate and wrap"},{"category":"section","location":"building/#Surfaces,-slabs-and-superlattices","page":"Building and editing","text":"The Build surface tool (Build → Surface, superlattice…) cleaves a slab of the current structure along (h k l) with a number of layers, vacuum and termination (auto cuts where the fewest bonds break), or builds an A/B superlattice of the current structure with a second structure file, with B optionally strained in plane to A.\n\nSuperlattices are stacked from bulk blocks: interface spacings come from the blocks and strained layers get no Poisson relaxation, so relax them.\n\nIn code: slab, surface_cell, add_vacuum, superlattice, stack_cells.","title":"Surfaces, slabs and superlattices"},{"category":"section","location":"building/#Nanotubes","page":"Building and editing","text":"Build → Nanotube… makes a single-wall (n, m) nanotube for any indices: n = m is armchair, m = 0 zigzag, anything else chiral, and a carbon tube is metallic when n − m is a multiple of 3. The preview gives the diameter, the period along the axis and the number of atoms before you create it; the tube opens in a new tab. It is periodic along c only (pbc F F T), with the chosen vacuum between neighbouring tubes in a and b, and as many periods along c as asked. The bond length defaults to graphite's C–C distance (1.4226 Å, from COD 9011577); two elements, e.g. B N, give a tube with one element per sublattice (enter the B–N distance as the bond length). The tube is an ideal rolled sheet, so relax it before use.\n\nIn code: nanotube, e.g. nanotube(6, 5) or nanotube(5, 5; periods=4, vacuum=15).","title":"Nanotubes"},{"category":"section","location":"building/#Bridging-atoms","page":"Building and editing","text":"Build → Oxygen bridge… (or Add oxygen bridge in the right-click menu) puts an atom into a bond. Select the two atoms of the bond, then a third atom (Shift+click, in that order).\n\nThe bridging atom (O by default; any element can be typed) goes to the middle of the bond plus a vector v of length offset (default 0.5 Å). v is perpendicular to the bond, in the plane of the three atoms, and points towards the third atom, so the new atom is inside their triangle.\nOther side of the bond (−v) places it at −v instead; Flip last bridge moves a bridge you just added to the other side.\nThe preview shows the two new bond lengths, the bridge angle, the closest other atom, and the largest offset that stays inside the triangle.\n\nThe bond is not stretched, so relax the result. With Si and the default offset, Si–O is 1.28 Å and Si–O–Si 134°. In code: bridge_position, add_bridge.","title":"Bridging atoms"},{"category":"section","location":"api_window/#API:-rendering-and-window","page":"Rendering and window","text":"","title":"API: rendering and window"},{"category":"section","location":"api_window/#Rendering","page":"Rendering and window","text":"","title":"Rendering"},{"category":"section","location":"api_window/#Window","page":"Rendering and window","text":"","title":"Window"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.reset_camera!-Tuple{Any, AtomBond.DisplayModel, ViewSettings}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"Default oblique view (settings.view_direction).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.reset_camera!"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.view_along!-Tuple{Any, AtomBond.DisplayModel, ViewSettings, Any}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"Apply a preset from view_direction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.view_along!"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.view_direction-Tuple{Lattice, Any}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"view_direction(lattice, which) -> (direction, up)\n\nPresets: :a, :b, :c look down a lattice vector; :astar, :bstar, :cstar look down a reciprocal vector, i.e. straight onto the (100), (010), (001) planes. A 3-vector is taken as a lattice direction [uvw].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.view_direction"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.draw_structure!-Tuple{Any, Crystal, ViewSettings}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"draw_structure!(lscene, crystal, settings) -> (DisplayModel, plots, atom_plot)\n\nDraw atoms, bonds and (optionally) the unit cell into an LScene. For a fully non-periodic structure (molecule) the box is drawn as dashed grey lines. Returns the plots so a caller can remove them and redraw without touching the camera, and the atom plot separately for picking.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.draw_structure!"},{"category":"constant","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.DISPLAY_STYLES","page":"Rendering and window","text":"Display styles, as in Materials Studio:\n\n:ball_and_stick — spheres scaled from covalent radii, cylinder bonds\n:cpk            — space-filling spheres at van der Waals radii, no bonds\n:stick          — bonds only; atoms are joints the size of the bonds\n:line           — bonds as thin lines; only unbonded atoms get a dot\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.DISPLAY_STYLES"},{"category":"type","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.ViewSettings","page":"Rendering and window","text":"ViewSettings(; kwargs...)\n\nDisplay settings of a viewer (v.settings), set by keyword, e.g. ViewSettings(style=:cpk, show_bonds=false). Change a field of an open viewer and call redraw!. Main fields: style (see DISPLAY_STYLES), ball_scale, bond_radius, show_bonds, bond_stubs, show_cell, show_boundary_atoms, wrap_atoms, show_labels, show_triad, orthographic, bond_lengths (:selected or :all), show_bond_angles, show_dihedrals, show_coordinates, show_charges, show_magmoms and bond_cutoffs (per element pair).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.ViewSettings"},{"category":"type","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.Viewer","page":"Rendering and window","text":"Everything a window needs to redraw itself when a setting changes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.Viewer"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.redraw!-Tuple{Viewer}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"Replace the structure plots after a settings change; the camera stays put.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.redraw!"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.save_view-Tuple{AbstractString, Viewer}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"save_view(path, viewer)\n\nSave just the 3D view (no panel, header or legend) as an image.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.save_view"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.set_crystal!-Tuple{Viewer, Crystal}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"set_crystal!(viewer, crystal; reframe=true, keep_selection=false)\n\nShow a different structure in the same window. This is not recorded for undo; edits from the panel go through edit!. The selection is cleared unless keep_selection (for edits that keep atom numbering).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.set_crystal!"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.snapshot-Tuple{AbstractString, Any}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"snapshot(outfile, crystal_or_path; kwargs...)\n\nRender to an image file (PNG) without opening a window.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.snapshot"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.visualize-Tuple{Crystal}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"visualize(crystal_or_path; settings=ViewSettings(), size=(1250, 960), panel=true, show=true) -> Viewer\n\nOpen a GLMakie window showing the structure, with a control panel on the right (panel=false hides it). Rotate with left-drag, zoom with the scroll wheel, pan with right-drag.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.visualize"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.visualize-Tuple{}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"visualize(; kwargs...) -> Viewer\n\nStart without a structure: an empty 5 Å cubic cell, with the New structure window open (a prototype such as diamond or wurtzite with your elements, or an empty cell to fill with Add). File → Open… and the library work as usual.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.visualize"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.open_library!-Tuple{Viewer, AbstractString}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"open_library!(viewer, entry or name) -> Bool\n\nReplace the structure with one from the library (undoable). It is Untitled until saved, so Save never writes into the library. box (vacuum in Å) puts a molecule into a periodic box for plane-wave codes (cubic box by default).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.open_library!"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.click_feedback!-Tuple{}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"click_feedback!(; flash=true, sound=true)\n\nTurn the visual click effects (button flash, menu flash, ripple in the 3D view) and the click sound on or off.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.click_feedback!"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.open_structure!-Tuple{Viewer, AbstractString}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"open_structure!(viewer, path) -> Bool\n\nOpen a structure file in a new tab (or in place of an untouched empty cell; a file already open in a tab is shown there).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.open_structure!"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.edit!-Tuple{Any, Viewer, AbstractString}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"edit!(f, viewer, what; reframe=false, keep_selection=false)\n\nReplace the crystal with f(crystal) as one undoable step. what describes the step for the status line (\"delete 2 atoms\"). Errors from f are shown in the status line and leave the structure unchanged. reframe re-aims the camera (for changes of cell size); keep_selection keeps the selected atoms (for edits that do not renumber them).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.edit!"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.redo!-Tuple{Viewer}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"Redo the last undone edit (Ctrl+Y). Returns whether there was one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.redo!"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.set_bond_angles!-Tuple{Viewer, Bool}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"set_bond_angles!(viewer, on)\n\nShow bond angles too, for the same bonds as the lengths (see set_bond_lengths!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.set_bond_angles!"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.set_bond_length_labels!-Tuple{Viewer, Bool}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"set_bond_length_labels!(viewer, on)\n\nShow or hide the bond-length labels (on by default), e.g. to see only charges or angles.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.set_bond_length_labels!"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.set_bond_lengths!-Tuple{Viewer, Symbol}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"set_bond_lengths!(viewer, mode)\n\nBond-length labels: :selected (bonds among 3 or more selected atoms) or :all (every bond).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.set_bond_lengths!"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.set_charge_labels!-Tuple{Viewer, Bool}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"set_charge_labels!(viewer, on)\n\nShow atomic charges (e) beside the selected atoms (or all atoms, see set_bond_lengths!). Charges come from the file: CASTEP .castep (Mulliken), LAMMPS data (charge/full style), extended XYZ, PDB.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.set_charge_labels!"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.set_coordinates!-Tuple{Viewer, Bool}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"set_coordinates!(viewer, on; system=nothing)\n\nShow atom coordinates beside the selected atoms (or all atoms, see set_bond_lengths!). system = :fractional, :cartesian or :auto changes the coordinate system.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.set_coordinates!"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.set_dihedrals!-Tuple{Viewer, Bool}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"set_dihedrals!(viewer, on)\n\nShow dihedral angles too, for the same bonds as the lengths (see set_bond_lengths!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.set_dihedrals!"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.set_magmom_labels!-Tuple{Viewer, Bool}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"set_magmom_labels!(viewer, on)\n\nShow magnetic moments (μB) beside the selected atoms (or all atoms, see set_bond_lengths!). Moments come from the file: CASTEP .castep (Mulliken spins) and .cell (SPIN=), extended XYZ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.set_magmom_labels!"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.undo!-Tuple{Viewer}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"Undo the last edit of the tab shown (Ctrl+Z). Returns whether there was one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.undo!"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.exit!-Tuple{Viewer}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"exit!(viewer; force=false) -> Bool\n\nClose the AtomBond window (File → Exit, Ctrl+Q). With unsaved changes the first Exit only warns in the status line; a second Exit (with no edits in between) closes without saving. Returns whether it closed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.exit!"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.save!-Tuple{Viewer}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"save!(viewer) -> Bool or the Save as tool\n\nSave to the current file, or open Save as when there is none (or it should not be overwritten).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.save!"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.save_to!-Tuple{Viewer, AbstractString}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"save_to!(viewer, path) -> Bool\n\nWrite the structure to path in the format given by its extension (or a PNG image of the view for .png). A structure file becomes the viewer's file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.save_to!"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.select_all!-Tuple{Viewer}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"Select every atom of the structure (Ctrl+A).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.select_all!"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.close_tool!-Tuple{Viewer}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"close_tool!(viewer; restore=true) -> Bool\n\nClose the tool open on the right; restore brings the view controls back. Returns whether a tool was open.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.close_tool!"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.open_tool-Tuple{Viewer}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"Key of the tool open on the right (:supercell, :shift, ...), or nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.open_tool"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.clear_selection!-Tuple{Viewer}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"Deselect all atoms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.clear_selection!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.select!","page":"Rendering and window","text":"select!(viewer, index, image=(0,0,0); add=false)\n\nSelect an atom programmatically (as a click would). add=true acts like Shift+click: toggles the atom in the current selection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.select!"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.active_tab-Tuple{Viewer}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"Number of the tab shown.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.active_tab"},{"category":"function","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.close_tab!","page":"Rendering and window","text":"close_tab!(viewer, k=active tab; force=false) -> Bool\n\nClose tab k. With unsaved changes the first call only warns in the status line; a second one (with no edits in between) closes it. Closing the last tab leaves an empty cell. Returns whether the tab closed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.close_tab!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.new_tab!","page":"Rendering and window","text":"new_tab!(viewer, crystal=empty cell; path=nothing)\n\nOpen crystal in a new tab and show it. Without a crystal: an empty cell, with the New structure tool open on the right.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.new_tab!"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.ntabs-Tuple{Viewer}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"Number of open tabs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.ntabs"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.open_in_tab!-Tuple{Viewer, Crystal}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"open_in_tab!(viewer, crystal; path=nothing) -> Bool\n\nShow a structure that was opened, built or taken from the library: in a new tab, or in place of an untouched empty cell. A file that is already open in a tab is shown there instead. path is the file it came from.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.open_in_tab!"},{"category":"method","location":"api_window/#AtomBond.switch_tab!-Tuple{Viewer, Integer}","page":"Rendering and window","text":"switch_tab!(viewer, k)\n\nShow the structure of tab k (its view, selection and undo history come back).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.switch_tab!"},{"category":"section","location":"formats/#File-formats","page":"File formats","text":"load_structure and save_structure choose the format from the file name.\n\nCode / program Read Write\nQuantum ESPRESSO pw.x input .in/.pwi (ibrav 0–12, -3, -12, 14; celldm or A…; if_pos); pw.x output .out/.pwo (last step) pw.x input\nABINIT .abi/.abinit (acell, rprim/angdeg, xred/xcart/xangst) .abi input\nSIESTA .fdf .fdf input\nFHI-aims geometry.in (atom, atom_frac) geometry.in\nVASP POSCAR, CONTCAR, *.vasp (selective dynamics); XDATCAR (last frame); vasprun.xml (final structure) POSCAR\nCASTEP .cell (with SPIN= and constraints); .geom (last frame); .castep output (final structure, charges, spins) .cell\nXCrySDen / VESTA XSF (crystal, slab, polymer, molecule) XSF\nGaussian and others cube (atoms and cell; the grid is skipped) –\nMaterials Studio .car, .xsd .car\nSHELX / AIRSS .res, .ins (with SYMM / LATT expansion) .res\nLAMMPS data file (.lmp, .data, data.*; atomic / charge / full) data file\nGeneral CIF (symmetry expanded), XYZ / extended XYZ, PDB (CRYST1) CIF (P1), extended XYZ, PDB\n\nA .in file is read as pw.x, FHI-aims or ABINIT input depending on its contents.\nFiles without a cell (XYZ, PDB without CRYST1, a molecule XSF, aims without lattice vectors) are put in a box with 5 Å of space and treated as molecules (not periodic). The box is drawn dashed.\nCIF: partial occupancies are drawn as full atoms, with a warning.\nExamples of each format are in examples/structures/formats. They are written by hand in each program's documented layout, not output of the programs themselves.\n\nThe separate input writers for DFT codes (with k-points and constraints) are described in DFT inputs and constraints. to_atomsbase and from_atomsbase convert to and from AtomsBase systems, which packages such as DFTK accept.","title":"File formats"},{"category":"section","location":"library/#Structure-library-and-element-data","page":"Library and element data","text":"","title":"Structure library and element data"},{"category":"section","location":"library/#Structure-library","page":"Library and element data","text":"File → Structure library… (Ctrl+L, also Build → From library…) lists known crystals and molecules with their formula, symmetry, cell and source (database entry and paper); Open loads one in a new tab. The structure is Untitled until saved, so Save never writes into the library. In code: structure_library, load_library (by name or formula), open_library!.\n\nFinding a structure: Show picks crystals, molecules or both; Group narrows the list to one group; Search keeps the structures whose name, formula, group or use contain all the words typed (ethane, WF6, halide, etch, precursor).\n\nGroup Structures Source\nSemiconductors: group IV Si, diamond, Si0.5Ge0.5 (ordered model) COD (Hom et al. 1975 precision lattice constants; Dismukes 1964 for SiGe)\nSemiconductors: III–V GaAs, AlAs, InAs, GaP, AlP, InP, GaSb, AlSb, InSb (zincblende); GaN, AlN, InN (wurtzite) 300 K lattice constants from Vurgaftman, Meyer, Ram-Mohan, J. Appl. Phys. 89, 5815 (2001); wurtzite u from Paszkowicz et al. (2003, 2004)\nOxides α-quartz SiO₂, corundum Al₂O₃, monoclinic HfO₂ and ZrO₂, rutile and anatase TiO₂, ZnO, MgO, β-Ga₂O₃, SrTiO₃ COD (published refinements, listed per entry)\nCarbon: graphite and graphene graphite (2H); graphene (one layer, periodic in-plane) COD (Trucano & Chen 1975, neutron diffraction); graphene from that lattice constant\nCarbon: nanotubes single-wall (5,5), (10,10), (9,0), (10,0), (6,5) Rolled graphene sheet with graphite's C–C distance: models, periodic along the tube axis only\nMolecules 99 molecules in the groups below Experimental gas-phase geometries from spectroscopy and electron diffraction, cited per molecule\n\nMolecules are grouped the way chemistry textbooks classify them: first organic (carbon compounds), organometallic (metal–carbon bonds) or inorganic, then by functional group or type of compound. Within a group they are in alphabetical order.\n\nClass Groups (examples)\nOrganic hydrocarbons (CH₄, C₂H₂, propyne, cyclopropane, C₆H₆); halocarbons (CF₄, CH₂Cl₂, CHCl₃); alcohols and ethers (CH₃OH, oxirane); carbonyl compounds: aldehydes, ketones, acids, esters, amides (H₂CO, HCOOH, ketene, methyl formate, formamide); nitrogen and sulfur compounds (HCN, CH₃CN, DMSO); heterocycles (pyridine, furan, thiophene); radicals (CH, CH₂, HCO, CF₃)\nOrganometallic metal alkyls (Al(CH₃)₃ and its dimer, Zn(CH₃)₂, In(CH₃)₃); metal carbonyls (Ni(CO)₄, W(CO)₆)\nInorganic elements (H₂, O₂, O₃, Cl₂); hydrides (H₂O, NH₃, SiH₄, B₂H₆, HF); oxides (CO, CO₂, SO₂, NO₂, SiO); nonmetal halides (BCl₃, SiCl₄, NF₃, SF₆); metal halides (TiCl₄, WF₆, Al₂Cl₆); radicals (NH₂, SiH₂, SiH₃, HO₂, SH, ClO)\nCarbon fullerenes (C₆₀)\n\nMolecules used in semiconductor processing also say what for (Used as): etch gas, dopant source, deposition precursor, ALD/CVD co-reactant (oxidant, nitrogen source) or solvent. Search finds them by that too.\n\nCrystals are CIFs from the Crystallography Open Database, stored as downloaded.\nIII–V semiconductors come from one review at 300 K instead, because COD's III–V entries are mostly old tables and some are off.\nMolecules are experimental gas-phase geometries: the bond lengths and angles of the cited papers and books (each value read in the authors' own text, never taken from a data compilation), from which scripts/molecules.jl computes the coordinates. Each entry gives its point group and references; a few carry a note where the structure is approximate. They open in a non-periodic box with 5 Å of space; the library's Molecules: in a periodic cubic box switch makes the box periodic for plane-wave codes.\nRadicals are open-shell species (surface adsorbates, plasma and CVD intermediates); their note gives the spin state to use.\nGraphene and nanotubes are periodic only along some directions (extended XYZ with pbc): graphene in-plane, a nanotube along its axis (c), with vacuum elsewhere. Nanotubes are ideal rolled sheets, so relax them before use.\nSiGe: the real alloy is random; the library holds the ordered model (Si and Ge on the two diamond sublattices) at the measured alloy lattice constant.\n\nThe library is data/library/library.toml with crystals/ and molecules/ beside it; scripts/build_library.jl downloads the COD files and rebuilds it, with the molecules from scripts/molecules.jl. Your own structures go into ~/.atombond/library/library.toml (or $ATOMBOND_HOME/library/), in the same format with the files next to it (category sets the group; an optional use is shown and searched).","title":"Structure library"},{"category":"section","location":"library/#Element-data","page":"Library and element data","text":"All element data is in one table, data/elements.csv (Z 1–103), generated by scripts/build_element_table.jl from the published sources:\n\nColumn Used for Source\nmass QE and LAMMPS masses NIST Atomic Weights and Isotopic Compositions (IUPAC/CIAAW standard atomic weights)\ncovalent_radius bonds, ball size Cordero et al., Dalton Trans. 2008, 2832\nvdw_radius_bondi CPK size Bondi, J. Phys. Chem. 68, 441 (1964), with Mantina et al. 2009\nvdw_radius_alvarez CPK size where Bondi has none Alvarez, Dalton Trans. 42, 8617 (2013)\ncolor_jmol atom colours Jmol element colours (H white, C grey, N blue, O red, …)\n\nelement(:Si) gives an Element; element_property(:Si, :vdw_radius_alvarez) reads any column.\n\nYour own values: put an elements.csv with the same layout in ~/.atombond/ (or $ATOMBOND_HOME), with only the columns and rows to change:\n\nsymbol,color,vdw_radius\nO,FF2020,1.40\nSi,1B3BFA,\n\ncolor and vdw_radius take precedence over the built-in columns, and new columns become readable with element_property. use_vesta_colors! takes the colours (and optionally the radii) from a VESTA elements.ini.","title":"Element data"},{"category":"section","location":"dft/#DFT-inputs-and-constraints","page":"DFT inputs and constraints","text":"","title":"DFT inputs and constraints"},{"category":"section","location":"dft/#Export","page":"DFT inputs and constraints","text":"File → Export DFT inputs… writes:\n\nQuantum ESPRESSO: a pw.x input, with ecutwfc and a pseudopotential file pattern such as {El}.UPF ({El} becomes each element symbol).\nABINIT: an .abi input (ABINIT 9 or newer) with ngkpt, ecut (the cutoff in Ha, half the Ry value entered) and pseudos from the pattern ({El}.psp8 by default); magnetic moments give nsppol 2 and spinat.\nSIESTA: an .fdf input with the k-grid, a DZP basis, PBE and MeshCutoff 300 Ry to adjust; pseudopotentials are read from <Element>.psf or .psml; magnetic moments give DM.InitSpin.\nVASP: POSCAR and KPOINTS in a folder, plus POTCAR.order (the species order; POTCARs are licensed, so AtomBond does not write them). A structure with constraints also gets INCAR.relax (ISIF), and one with magnetic moments INCAR.spin (ISPIN, MAGMOM).\nCASTEP: a .cell file.\nCIF (P1) or extended XYZ.\n\nThe written files have not been run through ABINIT or SIESTA by the tests (they are read back by AtomBond's own readers); check them with your version of the code.\n\nThe k-point mesh comes from a spacing in Å⁻¹ (2π included, n = ⌈|b|/Δk⌉); the tool shows the resulting mesh, and vacuum directions of slabs and molecules get one k-point. In code: kpoint_grid, write_qe, write_abinit, write_siesta, write_vasp_inputs, write_castep, write_cif, export_inputs.","title":"Export"},{"category":"section","location":"dft/#Cell-and-constraints","page":"DFT inputs and constraints","text":"Build → Cell and constraints… shows the cell (lengths, angles, volume, density, vectors) and sets constraints for geometry optimization.\n\nPeriodic box: a molecule can be put into a periodic box with a given vacuum (cubic or tight), so the cell is drawn and exported for plane-wave codes; Make it a molecule goes back. In code: periodic_box, as_molecule.\n\nLattice constraints: presets (all free, fixed cell, angles fixed, only c, a = b and c, volume only) or numbers for a b c and α β γ (0 = fixed, equal numbers = vary together). Not every code can express every combination:\n\nCode How\nQuantum ESPRESSO calculation = 'relax', or 'vc-relax' with cell_dofree (all, volume, xyz, z)\nABINIT ionmov 2 with optcell 0 (fixed), 1 (volume), 2 (all) or 6 (only c); other combinations are written as 2 with a note\nSIESTA MD.TypeOfRun CG, with MD.VariableCell T when the cell may change (the whole cell relaxes; other constraints are noted)\nVASP ISIF 2 / 3 / 7 in INCAR.relax; other combinations need a patched VASP\nCASTEP CELL_CONSTRAINTS, exact\n\nAtom constraints: fix the fractional x, y and/or z of the selected atoms (also Fix atoms / Free atoms in the right-click menu). Fixed atoms are marked × (+ when only some coordinates are fixed).\n\nCode How\nQuantum ESPRESSO if_pos flags. pw.x applies them to Cartesian force components, which is exact unless a partly fixed axis is oblique; the file then says so\nABINIT iatfix for fully fixed atoms; iatfixx/y/z for a fixed coordinate along a Cartesian axis (others are noted)\nSIESTA Geometry.Constraints for fully fixed atoms (partly fixed ones are noted)\nVASP POSCAR Selective dynamics\nCASTEP IONIC_CONSTRAINTS along the reciprocal directions\n\nPOSCAR, CASTEP .cell and pw.x input read the constraints back. Constraints stay through undo, shifts, rotations, wrapping and deletion, and supercells repeat them. In code: fix_atoms, free_atoms, with_constraints, describe_cell_constraints.","title":"Cell and constraints"},{"category":"section","location":"dft/#DFTK-and-AtomsBase","page":"DFT inputs and constraints","text":"to_atomsbase turns a structure into an AtomsBase system, which DFTK and other AtomsBase-aware packages take directly; from_atomsbase converts back. The conversion is tested; DFTK itself is not a dependency and is not tested here.\n\nusing AtomBond, DFTK\nsystem = to_atomsbase(load_library(\"Silicon\"))\nmodel  = model_DFT(system; functionals=PBE(), temperature=1e-3,\n                   pseudopotentials=PseudoFamily(\"dojo.nc.sr.pbe.v0_4_1.standard.upf\"))","title":"DFTK and AtomsBase"},{"category":"section","location":"api_building/#API:-analysis-and-building","page":"Analysis and building","text":"","title":"API: analysis and building"},{"category":"section","location":"api_building/#Analysis","page":"Analysis and building","text":"","title":"Analysis"},{"category":"section","location":"api_building/#Building","page":"Analysis and building","text":"","title":"Building"},{"category":"type","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.Bond","page":"Analysis and building","text":"A bond from atom i to the image of atom j displaced by shift lattice vectors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.Bond"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.bond_cutoff-Tuple{Symbol, Symbol}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"Longest distance at which elements a and b count as bonded: tolerance times the sum of covalent radii, but at least the sum + 0.3 Å (which only matters for light atoms: H–H in H2 is 0.741 Å, 1.15 × 0.62 = 0.713 Å; F–F in F2 is 1.414 Å, 1.15 × 1.14 = 1.311 Å).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.bond_cutoff"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.coordination-Tuple{Crystal, AbstractVector{Bond}}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"Number of bonds each atom takes part in.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.coordination"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.find_bonds-Tuple{Crystal}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"find_bonds(c; tolerance=1.15, min_length=0.4, metal_metal=:auto, cutoffs=Dict()) -> Vector{Bond}\n\nAtoms i and j are bonded when min_length < d ≤ max(tolerance * (r_i + r_j), r_i + r_j + 0.3 Å), with r the covalent radius. cutoffs can raise that limit for given element pairs, e.g. Dict((:C, :Ga) => 2.77) (keys in alphabetical order, as pairkey makes them). Bonds across periodic boundaries are included; each bond is listed once.\n\nCovalent radii of metals are large enough that cation–cation pairs in oxides and nitrides (Ti–Ti in rutile, Hf–Hf in HfO₂) would count as bonded. With metal_metal=:auto such bonds are kept only when every species is a metal (metals and alloys); pass true/false to force it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.find_bonds"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.bond_angle-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"Angle p–q–r at the middle point q, in degrees.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.bond_angle"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.dihedral-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"Dihedral (torsion) angle p–q–r–s in degrees, in (-180, 180]: the rotation about q→r that takes the p–q–r plane onto the q–r–s plane (IUPAC sign).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.dihedral"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.distance-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"Distance between Cartesian points p and q (no periodic images; see min_image_distance).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.measure-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"measure(points) -> (kind, value) or nothing\n\nDistance for 2 points, angle for 3 (at the middle one), dihedral for 4.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.measure"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.min_image_distance-Tuple{Crystal, Integer, Integer}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"min_image_distance(c, i, j) -> Float64\n\nShortest distance between atom i and any periodic image of atom j.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.min_image_distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.neighbor_pairs-Tuple{Crystal, Real}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"neighbor_pairs(c, cutoff) -> Vector{Tuple{Int,Int,Shift,Float64}}\n\nAll pairs (i, j, shift, distance) with |rj + L*shift - ri| ≤ cutoff, each pair listed once.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.neighbor_pairs"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.conventional_cell-Tuple{Crystal}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"conventional_cell(c; symprec=1e-3) -> Crystal\n\nStandard conventional cell for the space group (e.g. the 8-atom cubic cell of silicon), with symmetry-idealized positions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.conventional_cell"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.primitive_cell-Tuple{Crystal}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"primitive_cell(c; symprec=1e-3) -> Crystal\n\nSmallest cell with the same lattice (spglib's standardized primitive cell).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.primitive_cell"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.spacegroup-Tuple{Crystal}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"spacegroup(c; symprec=1e-3) -> (number, symbol) or nothing\n\ne.g. (227, \"Fd-3m\") for silicon. symprec is the position tolerance in Å.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.spacegroup"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.symmetry_dataset-Tuple{Crystal}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"symmetry_dataset(c; symprec=1e-3) -> Spglib.Dataset or nothing\n\nFull spglib dataset; nothing for structures that are not periodic in all three directions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.symmetry_dataset"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.add_bridge-Tuple{Crystal, Any, Any, Any}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"add_bridge(c, a, b, third; element=:O, offset=0.5, flip=false, min_distance=0.5) -> Crystal\n\nAdd a bridging atom (oxygen by default) between Cartesian positions a and b (two atoms, possibly periodic copies) at bridge_position with the third atom's position third fixing the plane and the side. Refuses a position closer than min_distance Å to any atom.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.add_bridge"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.bridge_position-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"bridge_position(a, b, c; offset=0.5, flip=false) -> Vec3\n\nCartesian position of an atom bridging bond a–b: the bond's midpoint plus offset Å perpendicular to the bond in the plane a b c (towards c, inside the triangle a b c), or minus it with flip=true (the other side of the bond).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.bridge_position"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.as_molecule-Tuple{Crystal}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"A periodic structure back to a molecule (not periodic; the cell is only a container).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.as_molecule"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.describe_cell_constraints-Tuple{Any}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"Words for cell constraints: \"a, b fixed; c free; angles fixed\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.describe_cell_constraints"},{"category":"function","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.fix_atoms","page":"Analysis and building","text":"fix_atoms(c, indices, axes=(true, true, true)) -> Crystal\n\nFix the fractional coordinates axes (x, y, z = along a, b, c) of the given atoms; the other coordinates of those atoms become free. axes = (false, false, false) frees them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.fix_atoms"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.free_atoms-Tuple{Crystal}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"Free all atom coordinates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.free_atoms"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.periodic_box-Tuple{Crystal}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"periodic_box(c; vacuum=10.0, cubic=false) -> Crystal\n\nPut a molecule (a structure that is not periodic) into a periodic box for plane-wave codes (Quantum ESPRESSO, ABINIT, ...): an orthorhombic cell vacuum Å larger than the molecule along x, y and z (cubic: all three equal), with the molecule in the middle. The result is periodic, so its cell is shown and exported; k-points along the vacuum directions are 1 (Γ).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.periodic_box"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.add_atom-Tuple{Crystal, Any, Any}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"add_atom(c, element, position; cartesian=false, min_distance=0.5) -> Crystal\n\nAdd one atom at a fractional (or, with cartesian=true, Cartesian Å) position. Refuses positions closer than min_distance Å to an existing atom, including periodic images.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.add_atom"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.closest_contact-Tuple{Crystal, Any}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"closest_contact(c, atoms; limit=200_000) -> (i, j, distance) or nothing\n\nThe closest pair between atoms and any other atom (periodic images included), e.g. to spot overlaps after moving a group. nothing when there is no other atom, or when checking would take more than limit pairs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.closest_contact"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.delete_atoms-Tuple{Crystal, Any}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"delete_atoms(c, indices) -> Crystal\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.delete_atoms"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.move_atoms-Tuple{Crystal, Any, Any}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"move_atoms(c, indices, shift) -> Crystal\n\nTranslate the given atoms by a Cartesian vector (Å).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.move_atoms"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.replace_species-Tuple{Crystal, Any, Any}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"replace_species(c, from, to) -> Crystal\n\nChange every atom of element from into to (e.g. all Ga → In).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.replace_species"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.rotate_atoms-Tuple{Crystal, Real, Any}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"rotate_atoms(c, angle, axis; center=centroid, atoms=all, images=nothing) -> Crystal\n\nRotate all atoms (or the indices in atoms) by angle degrees about the Cartesian axis through center (Å; default: the centroid of the rotated atoms). The cell stays fixed. images (one lattice shift per rotated atom) rotates those periodic copies instead, so a group that crosses a cell face turns as one piece.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.rotate_atoms"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.rotation_matrix-Tuple{Any, Real}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"rotation_matrix(axis, angle) -> 3×3 matrix\n\nRotation by angle degrees about the Cartesian axis (right-hand rule: counterclockwise when the axis points toward the viewer).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.rotation_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.set_position-Tuple{Crystal, Integer, Any}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"set_position(c, index, position; cartesian=false, min_distance=0.5) -> Crystal\n\nPut atom index at a fractional (or, with cartesian=true, Cartesian Å) position. Refuses positions closer than min_distance Å to another atom, including periodic images.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.set_position"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.substitute-Tuple{Crystal, Any, Any}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"substitute(c, indices, element) -> Crystal\n\nChange the element of the given atoms (e.g. a Ge substitution in Si).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.substitute"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.translate_atoms-Tuple{Crystal, Any}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"translate_atoms(c, vector; fractional=false, atoms=all) -> Crystal\n\nShift all atoms (or the indices in atoms) by vector: Cartesian Å, or fractional coordinates of a, b, c with fractional=true. Atoms may end up outside the cell; wrap maps them back.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.translate_atoms"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.nanotube-Tuple{Integer, Integer}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"nanotube(n, m; bond=1.4226, periods=1, vacuum=10.0, species=(:C, :C)) -> Crystal\n\nA single-wall (n, m) nanotube: the honeycomb sheet rolled along the chiral vector n a₁ + m a₂. The tube axis is c, periodic, with periods translational periods in the cell; a and b are square with vacuum Å between the walls of neighbouring images and are not periodic (pbc = (false, false, true)). bond is the nearest-neighbour distance before rolling (graphite's by default); species puts one element on each sublattice, e.g. (:B, :N) for a BN nanotube (give the B–N distance of hexagonal BN as bond). The tube is an ideal rolled sheet: relax it before use.\n\nnanotube(6, 5)                 # chiral, semiconducting, 364 atoms\nnanotube(5, 5; periods=4)      # armchair, 4 periods along c\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.nanotube"},{"category":"function","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.prototype_crystal","page":"Analysis and building","text":"prototype_crystal(name, elements, abc, angles=(90, 90, 90); title=\"\") -> Crystal\n\nA new structure: \"Empty cell\" (lattice abc, angles, no atoms) or one of the prototypes in PROTOTYPES (\"Diamond\", \"Zincblende\", \"Rocksalt\", \"Wurtzite\", ...) with elements (symbols, as many as the prototype needs) on its sites. Cubic prototypes use abc[1] only; hexagonal ones abc[1] and abc[3].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.prototype_crystal"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.supercell-Tuple{Crystal, Integer, Integer, Integer}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"supercell(c, n1, n2, n3) -> Crystal\nsupercell(c, P) -> Crystal\n\nRepeat the cell n1×n2×n3 times, or more generally build the cell with vectors (a', b', c') = (a, b, c) * P for an integer matrix P with det(P) > 0. For example P = [1 -1 0; 1 1 0; 0 0 1] gives the √2×√2 rotated cell.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.supercell"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.add_vacuum-Tuple{Crystal, Real}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"add_vacuum(c, vacuum) -> Crystal\n\nReplace c by a vector along the normal of the ab plane so that vacuum Å of empty space lie between the top and bottom atoms of neighbouring images. Atoms keep their Cartesian positions apart from a shift along the normal that centres them; in-plane coordinates are wrapped into the cell.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.add_vacuum"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.best_termination-Tuple{Crystal, Any}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"best_termination(c, hkl) -> shift\n\nTry a cut between every pair of neighbouring atomic planes of the (hkl) surface cell and return the shift that breaks the fewest bonds (ties: the lowest shift). For Si(111) this picks the cut between bilayers (one dangling bond per surface atom) rather than inside a bilayer (three).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.best_termination"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.slab-Tuple{Crystal, Any}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"slab(c, hkl; layers=4, vacuum=15.0, shift=:auto) -> Crystal\n\nCut a slab of layers (hkl) repeat units from bulk crystal c. One layer is one period of the surface cell along its normal (e.g. one cubic lattice constant for Si (001) from the conventional cell). With vacuum > 0 the c vector is made normal to the surface and vacuum Å of empty space separate the slab from its periodic image (the atoms are centred). shift chooses the termination (see surface_cell); :auto uses the cut that breaks the fewest bonds (best_termination).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.slab"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.stack_cells-Tuple{Vararg{Crystal}}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"stack_cells(cells...; strain=false, tol=0.01) -> Crystal\n\nStack crystals along their c vectors (each block shifted by the c vectors of the blocks below). The blocks must share the in-plane cell (a, b) within relative tolerance tol; with strain=true, every block is strained in plane to the first one (no Poisson relaxation along c).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.stack_cells"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.superlattice-Tuple{Crystal, Crystal, Any}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"superlattice(A, B, hkl; layers=(4, 4), strain=false) -> Crystal\n\nA/B superlattice along (hkl): layers[1] (hkl) repeat units of A followed by layers[2] of B, periodic in all directions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.superlattice"},{"category":"method","location":"api_building/#AtomBond.surface_cell-Tuple{Crystal, Any}","page":"Analysis and building","text":"surface_cell(c, hkl; shift=0) -> Crystal\n\nBulk cell equivalent to c with a, b in the (hkl) plane and c pointing out of it, in standard orientation (surface normal along z). shift (fraction of one layer spacing) moves the origin along c, which picks the termination of slabs cut from it. The structure is still periodic bulk.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.surface_cell"},{"category":"section","location":"gui/#Using-the-window","page":"Using the window","text":"","title":"Using the window"},{"category":"section","location":"gui/#Layout","page":"Using the window","text":"The window has a menu bar at the top, a row of tabs (one per open structure), the structure's formula, space group and title, the 3D view, and on the right either the view controls or one open tool. The line under the view describes the selection; the line below it shows messages, in red when something needs your attention.\n\nText boxes take effect when you click their button; pressing Enter first is optional. Three numbers can be separated by spaces or commas, and supercell repeats also by x (2x2x1).","title":"Layout"},{"category":"section","location":"gui/#Mouse","page":"Using the window","text":"Left-drag rotates, the scroll wheel zooms, right-drag pans.\nClick an atom to select it, Shift+click to add more: 2 atoms show their distance, 3 the angle at the middle atom, 4 the dihedral, in click order. Click empty space or press Esc to clear. Atoms outside the home cell are marked with a prime (Si1′).\nRight-click an atom for its menu: Edit atoms…, Change to X for each other element in the structure, Select all X, Delete, Fix / Free atoms, Shift / Rotate atoms…, Wrap atoms, Add oxygen bridge (three atoms selected) and Clear selection. The clicked atom is selected first unless it is already part of the selection, so select several atoms and right-click one of them to act on all of them.","title":"Mouse"},{"category":"section","location":"gui/#Menus","page":"Using the window","text":"Menu Items\nFile New structure… (Ctrl+N), Open… (Ctrl+O), Structure library… (Ctrl+L), Close tab (Ctrl+W), recent files, Save (Ctrl+S), Save as… (Ctrl+Shift+S), Export DFT inputs…, Save image…, Exit (Ctrl+Q)\nEdit Undo / Redo (named after the step), Select all (Ctrl+A), Clear selection, Delete selected atoms, Edit atoms…\nBuild New structure…, From library…, Cell and constraints…, Supercell…, Surface / superlattice…, Shift atoms…, Rotate atoms…, Oxygen bridge…, Wrap atoms into cell, Primitive / Conventional cell, Original structure\nView Reset view, Label all atoms and bonds / selected atoms only, Show / Hide bond lengths, bond angles, dihedral angles, atom charges, magnetic moments, atom coordinates, Coordinates: fractional / Cartesian, view along a, b, c, a, b, c*\n\nThe current file is shown at the right of the menu bar, with \"modified\" after unsaved changes.","title":"Menus"},{"category":"section","location":"gui/#View-controls","page":"Using the window","text":"The right-hand panel sets the display style (ball and stick, CPK, stick, line), switches bonds, bond stubs (half-bonds to atoms outside the cell), boundary atoms, the unit cell, atom labels and the axes, and sizes atoms and bonds. View along aims the camera along a, b, c, a*, b*, c* or any [u v w]; Orthographic switches the projection. The Cell and Edit selected atoms rows give quick access to supercells, primitive / conventional cells, and replacing, deleting, moving or adding atoms.","title":"View controls"},{"category":"section","location":"gui/#Tools","page":"Using the window","text":"The menu items that end in \"…\" (New structure, Structure library, Cell and constraints, Supercell, Surface, Shift atoms, Rotate atoms, Oxygen bridge, Edit atoms, Export) open in the right-hand column, in place of the view controls. Tools that change the structure say which tab they work on (\"on GaN.cif\") and follow you when you switch tabs. One tool is open at a time; its Close button (or close_tool!) brings the view controls back. Only the Open / Save file browser is a separate window.","title":"Tools"},{"category":"section","location":"gui/#Tabs","page":"Using the window","text":"Several structures can be open at once, one per tab. Open…, recent files, New structure and the library each open a new tab (except in place of an untouched empty cell, as visualize() starts); a file that is already open is shown in its tab. Every tab keeps its own structure, Original, undo history, selection, view and file, and edits, Undo and Save act on the tab shown.\n\nClick a tab to show it; Ctrl+Tab / Ctrl+Shift+Tab go to the next / previous tab.\n× or Ctrl+W closes a tab. With unsaved changes (marked \"*\" on the tab) the first close only warns; close again to discard them.\n+ starts a new structure.\n\nIn code: new_tab!, switch_tab!, close_tab!, ntabs, active_tab.","title":"Tabs"},{"category":"section","location":"gui/#Undo,-saving-and-exit","page":"Using the window","text":"Every change of the structure is one undoable step: Ctrl+Z undoes, Ctrl+Y (or Ctrl+Shift+Z) redoes, per tab.\n\nSave writes back to the current file when it is a POSCAR or XYZ file, or one AtomBond saved. A CIF or CASTEP file opened from disk is not overwritten (AtomBond writes only the cell and atoms and would drop the rest of such a file), so Save opens Save as instead, as it does for an untitled structure. Save as is a file browser with a file name and a format (see File formats), or a PNG image of the view. Replacing an existing file asks for a second click. Opened and saved files go into the recent-files list, kept in ~/.atombond/recent.txt (or $ATOMBOND_HOME).\n\nOpen (Ctrl+O) lists folders and the files AtomBond can read; click a file to see its formula and cell, double-click (or Enter) to open it. Arrow keys and Page Up/Down move the selection, Backspace goes up a folder.\n\nExit (Ctrl+Q) closes the window. With unsaved changes in any tab the first Exit only warns and names the tabs; Exit again closes without saving.","title":"Undo, saving and exit"},{"category":"section","location":"gui/#Click-feedback","page":"Using the window","text":"Buttons flash after a click, a chosen menu item flashes before the menu closes, and a click in the 3D view leaves a short ring at the cursor. A click sound plays if paplay is installed. click_feedback!(flash=false, sound=false) turns them off.","title":"Click feedback"},{"category":"section","location":"labels/#Labels-and-measurements","page":"Labels and measurements","text":"Selecting atoms measures them: 2 atoms give their distance, 3 the angle at the middle atom, 4 the dihedral, shown on the structure and in the line under the view. The View menu adds labels to the selected atoms, or to all atoms and bonds with Label all atoms and bonds (the same item switches back to Label selected atoms only).\n\nLabel View menu Shown With selected atoms\nBond lengths on by default (Hide bond lengths) Å, 3 decimals, blue, at the bond's midpoint; stubs to atoms outside the cell too 3 or more\nBond angles Show bond angles °, 1 decimal, green, inside the angle 4 or more\nDihedral angles Show dihedral angles °, signed (IUPAC), purple, on a ring around the central bond 5 or more\nCoordinates Show atom coordinates (x, y, z), below-right of the atom 1 or more\nCharges Show atom charges e, red positive, blue negative, above-left 1 or more\nMagnetic moments Show magnetic moments μB, below-left 1 or more\n\nFewer selected atoms than listed show the plain measurement instead. Torsions through a straight angle are left out, and −180° is shown as 180°. Coordinates are fractional for crystals and Cartesian (Å) for molecules; Coordinates: use Cartesian / fractional switches. A copy of an atom on a cell face shows its own position (e.g. 1.000).","title":"Labels and measurements"},{"category":"section","location":"labels/#Charges-and-magnetic-moments","page":"Labels and measurements","text":"AtomBond does not compute or guess charges; they come from the file, and atoms without one get no label.\n\nCharges: CASTEP .castep output (Mulliken, or Hirshfeld when there is no Mulliken table), LAMMPS data (charge and full styles), extended XYZ (a charge, charges or initial_charges column) and PDB formal charges. Extended XYZ and LAMMPS data save them.\nMoments: CASTEP .cell (SPIN=), CASTEP .castep output (Mulliken spins; one ħ/2 is 1 μB for g = 2) and extended XYZ (magmom, magmoms, initial_magmoms). They are saved to CASTEP .cell, extended XYZ, and by the VASP export as INCAR.spin with ISPIN = 2 and MAGMOM in POSCAR order.\n\nSupercells repeat charges and moments; changing an atom's element clears them. Symmetry treats atoms with different moments or charges as different, so an antiferromagnet keeps its magnetic cell: AFM bcc Cr is Pm-3m with 2 atoms, not Im-3m with 1 (examples/structures/formats/Cr_afm.cell).","title":"Charges and magnetic moments"},{"category":"section","location":"labels/#In-code","page":"Labels and measurements","text":"set_bond_lengths!(v, :all), set_bond_length_labels!, set_bond_angles!, set_dihedrals!, set_coordinates!, set_charge_labels!, set_magmom_labels!, or the corresponding ViewSettings fields, e.g. ViewSettings(bond_lengths=:all, show_bond_angles=true). For measurements on coordinates: distance, bond_angle, dihedral, min_image_distance.","title":"In code"},{"category":"section","location":"labels/#Bonds","page":"Labels and measurements","text":"Two atoms are bonded when their distance is at most 1.15 × the sum of their covalent radii (at least the sum plus 0.3 Å, so H₂ and F₂ are bonded). Metal–metal bonds are left out when the structure also contains non-metals (otherwise Ti–Ti in TiO₂ would show as bonds); pure metals and alloys keep them. The limit can be set per element pair with ViewSettings.bond_cutoffs, or find_bonds(c; cutoffs).","title":"Bonds"},{"category":"section","location":"#AtomBond.jl","page":"Home","text":"AtomBond is a crystal-structure builder and interactive ball-and-stick viewer written in Julia with GLMakie. It is meant for preparing ab-initio (DFT) calculations: open or build a structure, edit it, set relaxation constraints, and export input files for open-source codes such as Quantum ESPRESSO, ABINIT and SIESTA.\n\n(Image: AtomBond window showing a GaN wurtzite supercell)","title":"AtomBond.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Features","page":"Home","text":"Viewer: ball and stick, CPK, stick and line styles; bonds across periodic boundaries; views along lattice and reciprocal axes or any [uvw]; orthographic camera; PNG export; several structures in tabs.\nFormats: reads and writes CIF, XYZ, Quantum ESPRESSO, ABINIT, SIESTA, LAMMPS, XSF, PDB, SHELX .res and the input formats of several other codes; also reads cube files and DFT outputs (File formats).\nBuilding: supercells (including general matrices), primitive and conventional cells, surfaces and slabs, superlattices, molecules in a periodic box, shifting, rotating and wrapping atoms, bridging atoms (Building and editing).\nEditing: select atoms with the mouse, then delete, substitute, add or move them, with undo and redo.\nAnalysis: space group detection (spglib); labels for bond lengths, angles, dihedrals, coordinates, charges and magnetic moments (Labels).\nDFT inputs: fixed atoms and cell constraints, k-point meshes and spin (DFT inputs).\nStructure library: 131 crystals and molecules: Crystallography Open Database structures and experimental geometries from the literature, each with its reference (Library and element data).\n\n(Image: Methanol with bond lengths and angles)","title":"Features"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"AtomBond needs Julia 1.10 or newer and OpenGL 3.3 (any desktop; on Windows it also runs in WSL2). It is not registered yet, so install it from GitHub:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(url=\"https://github.com/QuantumWorm/AtomBond.jl\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Getting-started","page":"Home","text":"using AtomBond\nvisualize(\"structure.cif\")           # a file in any supported format\nvisualize(load_library(\"GaN\"))       # a structure from the built-in library\nvisualize()                          # nothing yet: build a new structure\n\nvisualize() opens an empty cell with the New structure tool on the right: pick a prototype (diamond, zincblende, rocksalt, wurtzite, perovskite, …) and type your elements and lattice constants, or keep the empty cell and add atoms one by one. Left-drag rotates, the scroll wheel zooms and right-drag pans. The first start takes a minute or two while Julia compiles GLMakie.\n\nFrom a script (julia script.jl), keep the window open with\n\nv = visualize(\"structure.cif\")\nwait(v.screen)\n\nThe same functions work without a window; see Working from Julia code.","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"#WSL2","page":"Home","text":"Under WSL2, AtomBond draws with software OpenGL, because the WSL GPU driver (Mesa D3D12) can freeze when many items are added to an open window, as when a tool opens. That is fast enough for typical cells. To use the GPU anyway, start Julia with ATOMBOND_GPU=1; setting LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE or GALLIUM_DRIVER yourself also takes precedence. Native Windows, macOS and Linux use the GPU.","title":"WSL2"},{"category":"section","location":"api_structures/#API:-structures-and-files","page":"Structures and files","text":"","title":"API: structures and files"},{"category":"section","location":"api_structures/#Structures-and-elements","page":"Structures and files","text":"","title":"Structures and elements"},{"category":"section","location":"api_structures/#Reading-and-writing-files","page":"Structures and files","text":"","title":"Reading and writing files"},{"category":"module","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond","page":"Structures and files","text":"AtomBond — build, edit and view crystal structures (ball-and-stick, GLMakie).\n\nLayers:\n\ncore/     Element data, Lattice, Crystal (no graphics)\nio/       File formats\nrender/   Draw a Crystal into a Makie scene\ngui/      Windows and interaction\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond"},{"category":"type","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.Crystal","page":"Structures and files","text":"Crystal(lattice, species, frac; pbc=(true, true, true), title=\"\", fixed, cell_constraints, charges, magmoms)\n\nA structure: a Lattice, element symbols species and fractional coordinates frac (one per atom). pbc marks the periodic directions (a molecule has none). Optional per-atom data: fixed (which fractional x, y, z are fixed in a geometry optimization), charges (e) and magmoms (μB), with NaN for unknown; cell_constraints follows CASTEP's CELL_CONSTRAINTS for a b c α β γ. Crystals are immutable: editing functions return new ones.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.Crystal"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.atoms_outside_cell-Tuple{Crystal}","page":"Structures and files","text":"atoms_outside_cell(c; atoms=all, tol=1e-3) -> Vector{Int}\n\nAtoms with a periodic fractional coordinate outside [0, 1), ignoring atoms within tol of a cell face (those are drawn on the face).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.atoms_outside_cell"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.cartesian_positions-Tuple{Crystal}","page":"Structures and files","text":"Cartesian positions of all atoms, in Å.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.cartesian_positions"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.crystal_from_cartesian-Tuple{Lattice, Any, Any}","page":"Structures and files","text":"crystal_from_cartesian(lattice, species, positions; kwargs...)\n\nLike Crystal(...) but takes Cartesian positions (Å) instead of fractional.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.crystal_from_cartesian"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.formula-Tuple{Crystal}","page":"Structures and files","text":"Chemical formula like \"Si8\" or \"Ga4As4\" (species in first-appearance order).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.formula"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.has_charges-Tuple{Crystal}","page":"Structures and files","text":"Whether any atom has a known charge.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.has_charges"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.has_constraints-Tuple{Crystal}","page":"Structures and files","text":"Whether any atom coordinate or lattice parameter is constrained.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.has_constraints"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.has_magmoms-Tuple{Crystal}","page":"Structures and files","text":"Whether any atom has a known magnetic moment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.has_magmoms"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.natoms-Tuple{Crystal}","page":"Structures and files","text":"Number of atoms.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.natoms"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.species_list-Tuple{Crystal}","page":"Structures and files","text":"Unique species in order of first appearance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.species_list"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.with_charges-Tuple{Crystal, Any}","page":"Structures and files","text":"with_charges(c, charges) -> Crystal\n\nc with other atomic charges (e; NaN or nothing = not known).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.with_charges"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.with_constraints-Tuple{Crystal}","page":"Structures and files","text":"with_constraints(c; fixed=c.fixed, cell=c.cell_constraints) -> Crystal\n\nc with other atom and/or cell constraints.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.with_constraints"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.with_magmoms-Tuple{Crystal, Any}","page":"Structures and files","text":"with_magmoms(c, magmoms) -> Crystal\n\nc with other magnetic moments (μB; NaN or nothing = not known).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.with_magmoms"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.with_positions-Tuple{Crystal, Any}","page":"Structures and files","text":"with_positions(c, frac; lattice=c.lattice, pbc=c.pbc) -> Crystal\n\nThe same atoms (species, constraints, charges, moments, title) at new fractional positions, optionally in a new cell.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.with_positions"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.wrap-Tuple{Crystal}","page":"Structures and files","text":"wrap(c; atoms=all, tol=1e-8) -> Crystal\n\nWrap atoms into the cell: map fractional coordinates into [0, 1) along periodic directions by adding whole lattice vectors (as ASE wrap, OVITO \"wrap at periodic boundaries\"). The structure itself does not change. atoms limits this to some atom indices; values within tol of 1 become 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.wrap"},{"category":"type","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.Element","page":"Structures and files","text":"Data of one element, from element(:Si): symbol, Z, name, mass (u), covalent_radius and vdw_radius (Å) and color. Other columns of the element table are read with element_property.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.Element"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.atomic_mass-Tuple{Any}","page":"Structures and files","text":"Standard atomic weight (u); throws for unknown species.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.atomic_mass"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.element-Tuple{Integer}","page":"Structures and files","text":"element(sym) -> Element\n\nLook up an element by symbol (:Si, \"si\", \"Si1\") or atomic number. Unknown symbols return a magenta placeholder rather than throwing, so that odd labels in input files still render.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.element"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.element_property-Tuple{Any, Symbol}","page":"Structures and files","text":"element_property(sym, column) -> Float64, String or nothing\n\nAny column of the element table for an element: :mass, :covalent_radius, :vdw_radius_bondi, :vdw_radius_alvarez, :color_jmol, :name, or a column added in an override file. Numbers are returned as Float64, empty values as nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.element_property"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.ismetal-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"Structures and files","text":"Whether element s is a metal (for leaving out metal–metal bonds in compounds).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.ismetal"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.override_elements!-Tuple{AbstractVector}","page":"Structures and files","text":"override_elements!(rows) / override_elements!(path)\n\nMerge rows (column => text, with a symbol column) into the element table: given columns replace the built-in ones; a new symbol needs Z. Affects structures drawn afterwards.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.override_elements!"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.use_vesta_colors!-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Structures and files","text":"use_vesta_colors!(path; radii=false)\n\nTake element colors (and, with radii=true, the van der Waals radii used for CPK) from a VESTA elements.ini (lines: number, symbol, atomic radius, van der Waals radius, ionic radius, R G B in 0–1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.use_vesta_colors!"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.vdw_radius-Tuple{Any}","page":"Structures and files","text":"Van der Waals radius (Å) used for CPK: Bondi, else Alvarez, else covalent + 0.8 Å.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.vdw_radius"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.Lattice-NTuple{6, Real}","page":"Structures and files","text":"Lattice(a, b, c, α, β, γ)\n\nBuild a lattice from cell lengths (Å) and angles (degrees), using the standard orientation: a along x, b in the xy plane.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.Lattice"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.Lattice-Tuple{AbstractVector, AbstractVector, AbstractVector}","page":"Structures and files","text":"Lattice(a, b, c)\n\nBuild a lattice from three cell vectors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.Lattice"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.cart_to_frac-Tuple{Lattice, AbstractVector}","page":"Structures and files","text":"Fractional coordinates of a Cartesian position r (Å).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.cart_to_frac"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.cell_vectors-Tuple{Lattice}","page":"Structures and files","text":"The lattice vectors a, b, c (Å).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.cell_vectors"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.cell_volume-Tuple{Lattice}","page":"Structures and files","text":"Cell volume in Å³ (negative for a left-handed cell).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.cell_volume"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.cubic-Tuple{Real}","page":"Structures and files","text":"Cubic lattice with edge a.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.cubic"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.frac_to_cart-Tuple{Lattice, AbstractVector}","page":"Structures and files","text":"Cartesian position (Å) of fractional coordinates f.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.frac_to_cart"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.lattice_parameters-Tuple{Lattice}","page":"Structures and files","text":"lattice_parameters(L) -> (a, b, c, α, β, γ)\n\nLengths in Å and angles in degrees.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.lattice_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.from_atomsbase-Tuple{Any}","page":"Structures and files","text":"from_atomsbase(system; title=\"\") -> Crystal\n\nAny AtomsBase system with three cell vectors (e.g. one returned by DFTK or AtomsIO).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.from_atomsbase"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.to_atomsbase-Tuple{Crystal}","page":"Structures and files","text":"to_atomsbase(c) -> AtomsBase.FlexibleSystem\n\nCell vectors and Cartesian positions in Å; periodicity from c.pbc.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.to_atomsbase"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.read_castep_cell-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Structures and files","text":"read_castep_cell(path or io; title=\"\") -> Crystal\n\nCASTEP .cell file: lattice from LATTICE_CART or LATTICE_ABC, atoms from POSITIONS_FRAC or POSITIONS_ABS (units ang, bohr, nm, ...; default Å). Other blocks and keywords are ignored.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.read_castep_cell"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.read_castep_geom-Tuple{Any}","page":"Structures and files","text":"read_castep_geom(path; frame=:last) -> Crystal\n\nOne frame of a CASTEP .geom file, by default the last (the relaxed structure); frame may also be an index (1 = starting geometry).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.read_castep_geom"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.read_castep_geom_frames-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Structures and files","text":"read_castep_geom_frames(path or io) -> Vector{Crystal}\n\nAll frames of a CASTEP .geom (or .md) file: cell (<-- h) and Cartesian positions (<-- R), converted from Bohr to Å. Each frame's title gives its step and energy (Hartree in the file, shown in eV).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.read_castep_geom_frames"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.read_castep_output-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Structures and files","text":"read_castep_output(path or io; charges=:mulliken) -> Crystal\n\nCASTEP .castep output: the last unit cell (\"Real Lattice(A)\") and the last \"Cell Contents\" fractional coordinates, i.e. the final structure of a geometry optimization, with atomic charges from the last population analysis: charges = :mulliken (the \"Charge (e)\" column of \"Atomic Populations (Mulliken)\"; Hirshfeld charges when there are no Mulliken ones), :hirshfeld, or :none. Magnetic moments come from the Mulliken \"Spin (hbar/2)\" column of spin-polarized runs (hbar/2 units = μB).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.read_castep_output"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.read_cif-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Structures and files","text":"read_cif(path) -> Crystal\n\nReads the first data block that defines a unit cell and expands the asymmetric unit to the full cell.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.read_cif"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.read_abinit-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Structures and files","text":"read_abinit(path or io) -> Crystal\n\nABINIT input: acell (Bohr unless a unit is given) with rprim or angdeg, natom, typat, znucl, and xred, xcart or xangst. Dataset-specific variables (acell2, ...) are ignored.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.read_abinit"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.read_aims-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Structures and files","text":"read_aims(path or io) -> Crystal\n\nFHI-aims geometry.in: lattice_vector lines (none for a molecule, which is boxed), atoms as atom x y z El (Å) or atom_frac f1 f2 f3 El.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.read_aims"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.read_siesta-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Structures and files","text":"read_siesta(path or io) -> Crystal\n\nSIESTA .fdf: ChemicalSpeciesLabel, LatticeConstant with LatticeVectors or LatticeParameters, AtomicCoordinatesFormat (Ang, Bohr, ScaledCartesian, Fractional / ScaledByLatticeVectors, NotScaledCartesianAng/Bohr) and AtomicCoordinatesAndAtomicSpecies. Without a lattice the atoms are boxed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.read_siesta"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.write_aims-Tuple{AbstractString, Crystal}","page":"Structures and files","text":"write_aims(path or io, crystal)\n\nFHI-aims geometry.in: lattice vectors and fractional positions (atom_frac); Cartesian atom lines without lattice vectors for a non-periodic structure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.write_aims"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.export_inputs-Tuple{Crystal, Symbol, AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"Structures and files","text":"export_inputs(c, format, dir, name; kspacing=0.25, ecutwfc=40, pseudo=\"{El}.UPF\") -> paths\n\nWrite c for format ∈ (:qe, :abinit, :siesta, :vasp, :castep, :cif, :xyz) into dir. VASP files go into a sub-folder name; the others are dir/name.<ext>. pseudo is the pseudopotential file pattern for Quantum ESPRESSO and ABINIT ({El} becomes the element; ABINIT gets .psp8 when the pattern is the default .UPF one).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.export_inputs"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.kpoint_grid-Tuple{Crystal}","page":"Structures and files","text":"kpoint_grid(c; kspacing=0.25, min_gap=5.0) -> (n1, n2, n3)\n\nMonkhorst–Pack / Γ-centred mesh for a k-point spacing in Å⁻¹ (2π included, n = ⌈|b|/Δk⌉; 0.25 is a reasonable default for semiconductors). Directions that are not periodic or that contain more than min_gap Å of vacuum get one k-point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.kpoint_grid"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.write_abinit-Tuple{AbstractString, Crystal}","page":"Structures and files","text":"write_abinit(path, c; kspacing=0.25, ecutwfc=40, pseudo=\"{El}.psp8\")\n\nABINIT input (.abi, ABINIT 9 or newer): the cell (acell 1 Å with rprim rows as the lattice vectors), natom, ntypat, znucl, typat, reduced coordinates xred, a Γ-centred ngkpt mesh, ecut in Ha (= ecutwfc/2, as ecutwfc is in Ry like Quantum ESPRESSO's) and pseudos from the pattern ({El} becomes the element symbol). Initial magnetic moments give nsppol 2 and spinat.\n\nConstraints: a relaxation (ionmov 2) with optcell for the cell (see abinit_optcell); fully fixed atoms are listed in iatfix, and a fixed fractional coordinate along a Cartesian axis in iatfixx/iatfixy/iatfixz (other partly fixed atoms are noted in a comment).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.write_abinit"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.write_castep-Tuple{AbstractString, Crystal}","page":"Structures and files","text":"write_castep(path, c; kspacing=0.25)\n\nCASTEP .cell file: Cartesian lattice in Å, fractional positions and an MP grid (CASTEP generates pseudopotentials on the fly when no SPECIES_POT block is given). Write the matching .param file yourself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.write_castep"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.write_cif-Tuple{AbstractString, Crystal}","page":"Structures and files","text":"write_cif(path, c)\n\nP1 CIF (all atoms listed, no symmetry operations beyond the identity).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.write_cif"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.write_kpoints-Tuple{AbstractString, Crystal}","page":"Structures and files","text":"write_kpoints(path, c; kspacing=0.25)\n\nVASP KPOINTS file with a Γ-centred automatic mesh (Γ-centring is safe for hexagonal cells as well).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.write_kpoints"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.write_qe-Tuple{AbstractString, Crystal}","page":"Structures and files","text":"write_qe(path, c; kspacing=0.25, ecutwfc=40, pseudo=\"{El}.UPF\", calculation=automatic)\n\npw.x input with ibrav = 0, the cell in Å and positions in crystal coordinates. pseudo is a file-name pattern in which {El} becomes the element symbol. ecutwfc is in Ry; check it against your pseudopotentials.\n\nConstraints: fixed atoms get if_pos flags (0 = fixed; pw.x applies them to Cartesian force components, which equals fixing fractional coordinates only when the cell axes are along x, y, z; a fully fixed atom is always exact). calculation is by default 'scf' without constraints, 'relax' for a fixed cell and 'vc-relax' with cell_dofree otherwise (see cell_constraints_qe).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.write_qe"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.write_siesta-Tuple{AbstractString, Crystal}","page":"Structures and files","text":"write_siesta(path, c; kspacing=0.25)\n\nSIESTA .fdf input: the species (pseudopotentials are read from <Label>.psf or .psml), the lattice vectors in Å, fractional coordinates, a Monkhorst–Pack k-grid, a DZP basis, PBE and MeshCutoff 300 Ry (adjust it). Initial magnetic moments give Spin polarized and DM.InitSpin.\n\nConstraints: a conjugate-gradient relaxation (MD.TypeOfRun CG) with MD.VariableCell when the cell may change (SIESTA relaxes the whole cell; other cell constraints are noted in a comment), and fully fixed atoms in Geometry.Constraints (partly fixed atoms are noted in a comment).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.write_siesta"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.write_vasp_inputs-Tuple{AbstractString, Crystal}","page":"Structures and files","text":"write_vasp_inputs(dir, c; kspacing=0.25) -> Vector of paths\n\nPOSCAR and KPOINTS in dir (created if needed). POTCAR is licensed and must be assembled by the user in the species order of the POSCAR, which is also written to POTCAR.order for reference.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.write_vasp_inputs"},{"category":"type","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.LibraryEntry","page":"Structures and files","text":"One structure of the library; file is an absolute path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.LibraryEntry"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.library_entry-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Structures and files","text":"The library entry called name (or with that formula), ignoring case.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.library_entry"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.load_library-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Structures and files","text":"load_library(name or entry) -> Crystal\n\nA structure from the library, by name (\"Silicon\") or formula (\"H2O\"). Molecules come in a box with 5 Å of space around them, as XYZ files do.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.load_library"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.structure_library-Tuple{}","page":"Structures and files","text":"structure_library(; kind=nothing) -> Vector{LibraryEntry}\n\nAll structures of the built-in library, followed by those of the user's library (~/.atombond/library/library.toml), if any. kind=:crystal or :molecule keeps one kind.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.structure_library"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.load_structure-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Structures and files","text":"load_structure(path) -> Crystal\n\nReads (format from the file name, see structure_format):\n\nVASP: POSCAR/CONTCAR/.vasp, XDATCAR (last frame), vasprun.xml (final structure)\nCASTEP: .cell, .geom (last frame, i.e. the relaxed structure), .castep output (final structure with Mulliken charges)\nQuantum ESPRESSO: pw.x input .in/.pwi, pw.x output .out/.pwo (last structure)\nFHI-aims geometry.in, SIESTA .fdf, ABINIT .abi/.abinit (and .in by content)\nCIF, XYZ / extended XYZ, XSF (XCrySDen, VESTA), Gaussian cube, PDB\nLAMMPS data (.lmp, .data, data.*), Materials Studio .car / .xsd, SHELX/AIRSS .res/.ins\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.load_structure"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.save_structure-Tuple{AbstractString, Crystal}","page":"Structures and files","text":"save_structure(path, crystal; kwargs...)\n\nWriter chosen by the file name: .cif (P1), .xyz/.extxyz, .in/.pwi (Quantum ESPRESSO, see write_qe), .abi/.abinit (ABINIT, see write_abinit), .fdf (SIESTA, see write_siesta), .xsf, .pdb, .lmp/.data/.lammps (LAMMPS data), geometry.in (FHI-aims), POSCAR/CONTCAR/.vasp, .cell (CASTEP, see write_castep), .car (Materials Studio), .res (SHELX/AIRSS). Keyword arguments (e.g. kspacing) go to the DFT input writers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.save_structure"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.structure_format-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Structures and files","text":"structure_format(path) -> Symbol or nothing\n\nWhich reader load_structure would use for this file name, or nothing: :cif, :xyz, :poscar, :xdatcar, :vasprun, :castep_cell, :castep_geom, :castep_out, :qe_in, :qe_out, :aims, :siesta, :abinit, :xsf, :cube, :pdb, :lammps, :car, :xsd, :res.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.structure_format"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.read_lammps_data-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Structures and files","text":"read_lammps_data(path or io; style=nothing) -> Crystal\n\nLAMMPS data file: box (with xy xz yz tilts), Masses and Atoms (atomic, charge or full style; taken from the \"Atoms # style\" comment or the number of columns). Each type's element is the element named in the Masses comment (1 28.0855 # Si), otherwise the one with the closest mass.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.read_lammps_data"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.read_pdb-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Structures and files","text":"read_pdb(path or io) -> Crystal\n\nPDB: the cell from CRYST1 (a structure without one, or with the 1 Å placeholder cell, is boxed as a molecule) and ATOM / HETATM records of the first MODEL. The element comes from columns 77–78, otherwise from the atom name; a formal charge in columns 79–80 (\"2+\", \"1-\") is kept.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.read_pdb"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.write_lammps_data-Tuple{AbstractString, Crystal}","page":"Structures and files","text":"write_lammps_data(path or io, crystal)\n\nLAMMPS data file, atomic style (charge style when the structure has charges; unknown ones are written as 0): the cell in LAMMPS' restricted triclinic form (a along x, b in the xy plane; tilts written when non-zero), one atom type per element with the element named in the Masses comment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.write_lammps_data"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.write_pdb-Tuple{AbstractString, Crystal}","page":"Structures and files","text":"write_pdb(path or io, crystal)\n\nPDB with CRYST1 (space group P 1) and HETATM records. Positions are Cartesian in the standard orientation (a along x, b in the xy plane), as PDB requires.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.write_pdb"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.read_car-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Structures and files","text":"read_car(path or io) -> Crystal\n\nMaterials Studio / Insight .car archive (version 3): the PBC line gives a b c α β γ, and atom lines give Cartesian Å positions with the element in the 8th column. Positions are taken in Materials Studio's default orientation (a along x, b in the xy plane). PBC=OFF files are boxed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.read_car"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.read_res-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Structures and files","text":"read_res(path or io) -> Crystal\n\nSHELX .res / .ins (AIRSS output included): CELL, LATT (centring, and inversion when positive), SYMM operations, SFAC element list and atom lines (name, SFAC number, fractional x y z). Symmetry-equivalent atoms are generated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.read_res"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.read_xsd-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Structures and files","text":"read_xsd(path or io) -> Crystal\n\nMaterials Studio .xsd document: the lattice from SpaceGroup (AVector, BVector, CVector) and the Atom3d elements (fractional XYZ in a periodic document, Cartesian Å otherwise). Image atoms (ImageOf) are skipped; for a mixed-occupancy site the first component is used.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.read_xsd"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.write_car-Tuple{AbstractString, Crystal}","page":"Structures and files","text":"write_car(path or io, crystal)\n\nMaterials Studio .car archive (PBC=ON with a b c α β γ and P1, or PBC=OFF), Cartesian positions in the standard orientation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.write_car"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.write_res-Tuple{AbstractString, Crystal}","page":"Structures and files","text":"write_res(path or io, crystal)\n\nSHELX .res in P1 (LATT -1, no SYMM), as AIRSS writes it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.write_res"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.read_poscar-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Structures and files","text":"read_poscar(path) -> Crystal\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.read_poscar"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.write_poscar-Tuple{AbstractString, Crystal}","page":"Structures and files","text":"write_poscar(path, crystal; cartesian=false)\n\nWrite a VASP 5 POSCAR. Atoms are grouped by species (in first-appearance order).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.write_poscar"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.read_qe-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Structures and files","text":"read_qe(path or io) -> Crystal\n\npw.x input: ibrav 0 with CELLPARAMETERS (alat, bohr or angstrom) or ibrav 1–12, -3, -12, 14 with celldm or A, B, C, cos...; ATOMICPOSITIONS in crystal, alat, bohr or angstrom. Species labels map to elements via their name (Fe1 → Fe).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.read_qe"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.read_qe_output-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Structures and files","text":"read_qe_output(path or io) -> Crystal\n\npw.x output: the last CELLPARAMETERS / ATOMICPOSITIONS printed during a relax or vc-relax (the final structure), otherwise the input structure listed in the header.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.read_qe_output"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.read_vasprun-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Structures and files","text":"read_vasprun(path or io) -> Crystal\n\nThe final structure of a vasprun.xml (finalpos, or the last structure written when the run did not finish).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.read_vasprun"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.read_xdatcar-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Structures and files","text":"read_xdatcar(path or io; frame=:last) -> Crystal\n\nOne frame of an XDATCAR (by default the last). Variable-cell runs, which repeat the header before every frame, are handled.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.read_xdatcar"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.read_cube-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Structures and files","text":"read_cube(path or io) -> Crystal\n\nGaussian cube file: the atoms and the cell spanned by the grid (Bohr, or Å when the grid counts are negative). The volumetric data is not read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.read_cube"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.read_xsf-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Structures and files","text":"read_xsf(path or io) -> Crystal\n\nXSF file: CRYSTAL / SLAB / POLYMER (periodic in 3 / 2 / 1 directions, cell from PRIMVEC, atoms from PRIMCOORD) or MOLECULE (ATOMS, boxed). Atoms may be given by symbol or atomic number; forces after the coordinates are ignored. For animated files (ANIMSTEPS) the last step is read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.read_xsf"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.write_xsf-Tuple{AbstractString, Crystal}","page":"Structures and files","text":"write_xsf(path or io, crystal)\n\nXSF with PRIMVEC and PRIMCOORD (Cartesian Å): CRYSTAL, SLAB or POLYMER after the periodic directions, MOLECULE (ATOMS) for a non-periodic structure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.write_xsf"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.read_xyz-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"Structures and files","text":"read_xyz(path; padding=5.0) -> Crystal\n\nReads the first frame only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.read_xyz"},{"category":"method","location":"api_structures/#AtomBond.write_xyz-Tuple{AbstractString, Crystal}","page":"Structures and files","text":"write_xyz(path, crystal)\n\nWrite extended XYZ with the cell in the comment line.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AtomBond.write_xyz"}]
}
